1.2.30 Final Mastery Examination

The source's mastery gate requires understanding primitives, wrappers, conversion, operators, control flow, switch, methods, pass-by-value, scope, arrays, Strings, null, final, static, initialization, this/super, packages, access modifiers, enums, varargs, recursion and assertions, plus debugging, performance, production and implementation. 

I would make the final test approximately:

Part A — Theory

40 questions

Part B — Conceptual

30 questions

Part C — Output Prediction

30 questions

Part D — Code Debugging

20 questions

Part E — Implementation

10 coding problems

Part F — JVM/Internals

20 questions

Part G — Production Design

20 scenarios

Part H — Senior Interview

20 questions

So Module 1.2 becomes far more than memorizing definitions.


---

The standard we'll maintain for every remaining module

For example, when we reach OOP, equals()/hashCode(), SOLID, final, inheritance, constructors, annotations, etc., every individual subtopic will have:

Layer	What you'll be tested on

Basic	What is it?
Intermediate	How is it used?
Advanced	How does it work internally?
Conceptual	Why does Java behave this way?
Theory	Exact rules/contracts
Implementation	Build it
Output	Predict it
Trap	Find the misconception
Debugging	Find the bug
Performance	Runtime implications
Production	Real-world usage
Design	Choose between alternatives
Senior	Defend the engineering decision


That is exactly the progression your module's completion standard requires: explain → implement → internals → edge cases → trade-offs → debug → production scenario. 

And the final state should be not merely "I studied Java", but:

> I can explain it, code it, predict it, debug it, understand what the JVM is doing, recognize the traps, and make the correct production design decision.



That is the level we should maintain for Modules 1.3 through 1.9 as well.
